# Climbs and descents, in a light aeroplane: up at the climbing speed, level
# off, and back down at a steady rate.
#
#   name TEXT...                   what the lesson is called
#   teaches CLASS                  which class of aircraft it is for
#   stage TEXT...                  a stage, in the order flown
#   do TEXT...                     what to do at this stage
#   until PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE     what ends the stage
#   hold PROPERTY LOW HIGH TEXT... a band that must hold for the whole stage
#   need PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE TEXT...  what must be true by the stage's end
#
# with `#` beginning a comment; src/sim/lesson.hpp owns the format.
#
# **The speeds are each aeroplane's own**: `climb` is the speed its published
# climb rate was measured at, which `sim::departure_speeds` reads from
# `assets/figures/<id>.xml`. The four light aircraft climb between about 48
# knots and about 80, so a number here would teach the wrong speed to three
# of them.
#
# **The heights are against where the stage began**, so the lesson can be
# flown at any altitude: `start+1000` is a thousand feet above wherever this
# was started, not a thousand feet above the sea.

name Climbs and descents in a light aeroplane
teaches light-aircraft

stage The climb
do Set the climbing power and let the speed settle at the climbing speed
do Hold the nose where the speed sits right: look out, and lower it to see ahead
hold velocities/vc-kts climb-15 climb+15 Hold the climbing speed
until position/h-agl-ft >= start+900

stage Levelling off
do Lower the nose at the height you want and let the speed build
do Set the cruising power once the speed is there
until velocities/h-dot-fps <= 3.0

stage The descent
do Bring the power back and let the nose down to a steady rate of descent
do Hold the speed and keep the engine warm
hold velocities/vc-kts climb-15 climb+45 Hold the speed in the descent
until position/h-agl-ft <= start-500
